Setting. For a topological space $X$, let $\mathcal{B}$ denote the family of countable Borel covers of $X$, $\mathcal{B}_\Omega$ the family of countable Borel $\omega$-covers (covers such that every finite subset of $X$ is contained in some member of the cover, and $X$ is not a member), and $\mathcal{B}_\Gamma$ the family of countable Borel $\gamma$-covers (infinite covers in which every $x\in X$ belongs to all but finitely many members). For families $\mathscr{A},\mathscr{B}$ of covers, consider the standard selection principles
- $\mathsf{S}_1(\mathscr{A},\mathscr{B})$: for every sequence $(U_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ in $\mathscr{A}$ there exist $U_n\in U_n$ with $\{U_n:n\in\mathbb N\}\in\mathscr{B}$;
- $\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\mathscr{A},\mathscr{B})$: for every sequence $(U_n)$ in $\mathscr{A}$ there exist finite $\mathcal F_n\subseteq U_n$ with $\bigcup_n\mathcal F_n\in\mathscr{B}$;
- $\mathsf{U}_{\mathrm{fin}}(\mathscr{A},\mathscr{B})$: for every sequence $(U_n)$ in $\mathscr{A}$ with no finite subcover there exist finite $\mathcal F_n\subseteq U_n$ with $\{\bigcup\mathcal F_n:n\in\mathbb N\}\in\mathscr{B}$.

Applied to the Borel families $\mathcal{B},\mathcal{B}_\Omega,\mathcal{B}_\Gamma$, these selection principles, together with the Hurewicz, Menger and Rothberger style variants, yield finitely many provably distinct classes whose ZFC-implication diagram (the Borel-cover Scheepers diagram) has been completely determined. A "configuration" is an assignment, to each class in the diagram, of either "satisfied" or "not satisfied" by a single space $X\subseteq\mathbb R$, that is consistent with the ZFC-provable implications.

Problem. Working under Martin's Axiom, or under any strictly weaker hypothesis (for example, $\mathfrak{p}=\mathfrak{c}$, $\operatorname{cov}(\mathcal{M})=\mathfrak{c}$, $\mathfrak{b}=\mathfrak{d}$, etc.), construct a set of reals $X$ realizing each consistent configuration of the Borel-cover classes other than the four configurations already known to be realizable from the established constructions. Equivalently, for every consistent configuration not yet realized, exhibit (under such a hypothesis) a separable metrizable space witnessing precisely that configuration.

